@node Differential Motivation
@section Motivation

Differential dataflow programs look like many standard big-data
computations, borrowing idioms from frameworks like MapReduce and SQL.
The difference is what happens after you run one: you can @emph{change}
the data inputs, and differential dataflow promptly reports the
corresponding changes in its output.  This simple setup, write a program
and then change its input, opens up a surprising breadth of scalable
computation, because the system maintains the result incrementally
rather than recomputing it from scratch.

A differential program is built from @dfn{collections}, multisets whose
contents change over logical time, and operators over them
(@pxref{Collections}).  The operators (@code{map}, @code{filter},
@code{join}, @code{reduce}, @code{iterate} and the rest) build new
collections from old ones.  Underneath, each collection is a timely
stream of @code{(record, time, diff)} updates, so a change to an input
flows through as diffs, and only the records whose multiplicity actually
changed are recomputed.
